feat: pack the hero pattern tighter and smaller

The first pass read as scattered ornaments rather than texture: 39
marks, most of them large, with wide gaps between them. This replaces
it with 323 marks at roughly half the size, packed so only a few
pixels separate neighbours.

Most marks now sit at 7-14 units against the previous 18-46, with a
scattering of larger ones around 19-26 left as focal points. The
stroke drops from 3 to 1.6 to match: at this scale the old weight was
nearly as thick as the smallest marks are wide, and every shape closed
into a blob.

Placement is grid-based with per-cell jitter, which keeps the spacing
even without reading as a grid, and every candidate is collision-
checked against what is already down so no two marks overlap. The
density gradient is unchanged in intent — sparse behind the headline,
filling in toward the right — but now expressed as a probability per
cell rather than by hand-placing each mark.

The vocabulary and the rules around it are unchanged: hexagons at
roughly one in five, some partial, never tiled into honeycomb; the
same nine mark types; dots as the only filled shapes, tucked into the
gaps left over. Coordinates were generated once by a seeded pass and
are frozen here as literals, so nothing random runs in the browser and
the layout is identical on every load.

Coverage in the empty field right of the headline rises from 5,830 to
9,272 pixels. Below 640px the stroke thins to 1.3 and the 148 marks
tagged dense are hidden, leaving 175 — half the density behind type
that fills the screen.
